WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.960
From Kim's uncertain future to the fate&nbsp;
of various "Breaking Bad" characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.427
here's what we're still wondering about&nbsp;
after the final season of "Better Call Saul".

00:00:08.427 --> 00:00:14.820
Of all places, why did the wealthy criminal lawyer&nbsp;
escape to the cold and unforgiving land of Omaha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.640
Nebraska? Was it just an obscure location to avoid&nbsp;
being captured? After all, it almost was as Gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.500
does go unnoticed for a matter of time before&nbsp;
former Albuquerque cab driver Jeff recognizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.880
him. However, this location is actually both&nbsp;
a reference to a throwaway line from "Breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.220
Bad" and an important place from Kim Wexler's&nbsp;
past. Saul tells Walt as they hide out under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.716
vacuum repair shop in the "Breaking Bad" episode&nbsp;
"Granite State" a prediction of his future.

00:00:39.716 --> 00:00:44.280
"If I'm lucky, a month from now, best-case&nbsp;
scenario, I'm managing a Cinnabon in Omaha."

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.920
This scene is revisited in the series finale of&nbsp;
"Better Call Saul" and also serves as a set-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.120
for the prequel series. The sad fulfillment&nbsp;
of this line is accompanied by a gorgeous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.980
black and white composition that captures the&nbsp;
emptiness of the icing-slinging Gene Takovic.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.140
However, that may not be the only reason Saul&nbsp;
hides out here. As we learn in Season 6, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.120
Kim's flashback earring thievery, she's actually&nbsp;
from Omaha, as seen on her mother's license plate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.566
Was Saul originally hoping Kim may have returned&nbsp;
here and chosen Nebraska in hopes of a reunion?

00:01:14.566 --> 00:01:18.780
Before we flash-forward to full Saul, we&nbsp;
witness the devastating breakup of Jimmy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.700
and Kim. Once again, the exact timeline is a&nbsp;
little unclear. It's hard to pinpoint just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.260
long it's been since Kim made that difficult&nbsp;
decision due to the artful way in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.220
series portrays the passage of time. So the&nbsp;
question is — how long was Jimmy left alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.120
before he received a fat check from a&nbsp;
case he first discovered in Season 1?

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.100
It would be interesting to know how long Jimmy&nbsp;
was forced to sit with himself before Saul crept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.600
up and fully took over his persona and life.&nbsp;
Who gave him the call updating him on the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.640
now that Hamlin, Hamlin &amp; McGill is fully&nbsp;
dissolved? Was it a reluctant conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.600
with a Davis &amp; Main representative? Or&nbsp;
could it possibly have been Erin Brill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.284
his old co-worker who kept a close&nbsp;
eye on Jimmy during his time there?

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:03.840
Manuel Varga — Nacho's father — played a pivotal&nbsp;
role in his son's final episodes. The automobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.580
upholstery shop owner and upstanding citizen&nbsp;
was the epitome of good in a series full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.140
of morally gray characters and others who&nbsp;
have intentionally broken bad. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.180
Nacho was able to share a tear-jerking goodbye&nbsp;
with his father before he met his untimely demise.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.920
However, the question remains — what was the fate&nbsp;
of Manuel? The last we saw of Nacho's father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.420
Mike paid him a visit to let him know that&nbsp;
his son died a good man. Thematically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.260
Mike gave Manuel a sense of closure&nbsp;
Mike will never have while providing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.280
viewers with another redeeming moment for&nbsp;
the ex-officer and further revealing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.320
honorable true nature. While we may never see&nbsp;
Manuel again, thanks to Mike he will at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.760
have some sense of closure as he presumably&nbsp;
continues to work in his shop. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.052
without Nacho around Manuel will be safe from the&nbsp;
cartel as he can no longer be used as leverage.

00:02:50.052 --> 00:02:56.058
"If you are true to your word, there will be&nbsp;
no reason for anyone to harm your father."

00:02:56.058 --> 00:03:00.540
There were very few joyful moments&nbsp;
in the grim series finale. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.020
Kim Wexler remains one of the show's&nbsp;
most hopeful characters who still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.860
seems worthy of true redemption. In&nbsp;
a strict exercise of self-discipline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.360
Kim settles for a simple life living in&nbsp;
Florida working at Palm Coast Sprinklers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.520
where she creates brochures and catalogs for the&nbsp;
company. After her explosive call with Saul, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.080
takes accountability for her actions by confessing&nbsp;
to her part in Howard's death and volunteering at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.300
her local free legal clinic. Kim soon returns to&nbsp;
her true nature by burying herself in paperwork.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.160
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.840
Rhea Seehorn breaks down this turn of events&nbsp;
and what it might mean for Kim's future, saying,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.960
"I think she's thinking two things:&nbsp;
One, that it's possible that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.200
allow myself to find some joy and&nbsp;
passion in my life again. And two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.060
that it's possible that I can atone for my actions&nbsp;
in an active way, instead of just a passive way."

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.880
She expands on Kim's more authentic altruism&nbsp;
and even teases a possible future reunion with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.860
Jimmy — all of which is tied into the cathartic&nbsp;
decision to acknowledge her past mistakes.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.300
The double-edged sword with Kim finding&nbsp;
happiness again is there is now someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.740
out there who wants to snatch it away. After Kim&nbsp;
remorsefully unveils her role in Howard's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:09.180
she brings her completed statement to his&nbsp;
widow in an attempt to make amends. Cheryl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.140
Hamlin is understandably livid at this&nbsp;
revelation and threatens to sue Kim in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.040
civil court for everything she has in&nbsp;
the event that Kim avoids prosecution.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.180
With the overall lack of evidence or witnesses&nbsp;
available to implicate Kim in Howard's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.860
it seems unlikely that she'll be prosecuted,&nbsp;
especially after Jimmy clears her name in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.940
series finale. However, should Cheryl learn&nbsp;
that Kim has re-entered the law and is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.680
on speaking terms with her convicted ex-husband&nbsp;
Jimmy, the wronged widow may seek vengeance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.480
This could involve taking Kim to civil court&nbsp;
as promised and even exposing her past to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.511
new associates in Florida, potentially&nbsp;
shattering Kim's fragile fresh start.

00:04:43.511 --> 00:04:47.100
After Jimmy weaponizes the&nbsp;
law in Saul's signature style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.780
he has a sudden change of heart after seeing&nbsp;
Kim. However, this ultimately lands him in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:55.800
far less luxurious prison than he originally&nbsp;
worked out for himself. His initial sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.720
deal of seven years is increased to a crushing&nbsp;
sentence of 86 after his astonishing admission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.280
of guilt, in which he details his actions&nbsp;
in shielding Heisenberg from prison during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.780
his crime reign. He protects Kim and even&nbsp;
displays remorse for not doing more to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.100
his brother Chuck and admits the role he played&nbsp;
in the events that led to his brother's tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.060
death. As star Bob Odenkirk stated during&nbsp;
an interview with Entertainment Weekly,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.700
"It's the bravest, most honest thing he can do."

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:25.800
The series finale sees Jimmy return to using&nbsp;
his birth name while serving his sentence at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.260
very prison he wanted to avoid: ADX Montrose, as&nbsp;
indicated by the letters on his jumpsuit. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.120
ADX Montrose may not be the final stop for&nbsp;
Jimmy, as Rhea Seehorn offers a ray of hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.560
with Entertainment Weekly that suggests he could&nbsp;
be seeing more of his ex-wife. Seehorn explained,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.160
"I think she's going to go through a&nbsp;
very complicated process of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.640
figure out if she can help decrease&nbsp;
his sentence in a way that is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.194
just. And I don't think that's the last time&nbsp;
they'll see each other. Not by a long shot."

00:05:52.194 --> 00:05:55.740
The last three episodes of "Better&nbsp;
Call Saul" intertwine with the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.640
series in some interesting ways. In&nbsp;
the Season 6's third to last episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.800
aptly titled "Breaking Bad," Gene catches up&nbsp;
with his old secretary Francesca, played by Tina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.260
Parker. She updates him on the fate of a few of&nbsp;
Walt's old accomplices. The first is Walt's widow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.640
Skyler White, who presumably got her deal&nbsp;
with the DEA, although she also mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.680
that Jesse Pinkman's abandoned El Camino was&nbsp;
found somewhere near the border of Mexico. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.940
where are Skyler and her son, Walter Jr., now?&nbsp;
When viewers last saw them in the "Breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.600
Bad" series finale, they were living in&nbsp;
a small apartment and attempting to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.100
quiet lives. That could be the reason why they&nbsp;
didn't accompany Marie to face Jimmy in court.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.560
Walter Jr. is in for some good news though,&nbsp;
as he still has the $9.7 million coming for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.120
his 18th birthday from Walter's leftover&nbsp;
drug money in the form of an irrevocable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.480
Trust from Elliot and Gretchen's&nbsp;
company, Grey Matter Technologies.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.440
Marie's unexpected appearance in the&nbsp;
finale was an enormous delight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.520
both the fans and crew. "Better Call&nbsp;
Saul" co-creator Peter Gould praised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.300
actress Betsy Brandt's return during&nbsp;
an interview with Variety. He said,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.500
"I was so grateful that she was willing to come&nbsp;
to Albuquerque. She has a couple of scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.420
so she was there for quite a while. Working with&nbsp;
her reminded me of what a brilliant actress she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.780
is, and every time she did her scenes, she&nbsp;
would bring something a little different."

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.020
Viewers can also be confident that&nbsp;
underneath all that beautiful black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:17.040
and white footage used in the final episode&nbsp;
of "Better Call Saul," Marie was draped in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.340
her signature purple while delivering&nbsp;
on of the best lines of the series:

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:27.751
"They tell me they found you in a garbage&nbsp;
dumpster. Well, that makes sense."

00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:32.880
When "Breaking Bad" ended in 2013, most&nbsp;
fans thought we'd seen the last of Walt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.900
and Jesse. However, even then there was some&nbsp;
comfort in knowing that the universe would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.040
continue with the already announced prequel&nbsp;
spin-off about the loudmouth criminal lawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.060
Saul. However, now we are facing the&nbsp;
possible conclusion of the entire franchise.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.120
Still, there is a shred of hope&nbsp;
remaining thanks to some recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.020
comments that Peter Gould made to The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter which suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.900
this may not be the end of the&nbsp;
"Breaking Bad" saga. Gould said,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.820
"Look, if Vince or I or any of the folks&nbsp;
who worked on the show had an idea that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:04.320
were excited about, never say never. Vince&nbsp;
and I both feel like we want and need to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.740
something different and change things up.&nbsp;
If we ever did come back to this universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.520
hopefully we bring a new set of ideas that would&nbsp;
enrich it and give us something different."

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.160
Deadline recently reported&nbsp;
that Vince Gilligan's next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.400
show is going to be an Apple TV+&nbsp;
exclusive starring Rhea Seehorn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.080
but isn't expected to be related to the&nbsp;
stories of Better Call Saul and Breaking Bad.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.200
While this is certainly an exciting&nbsp;
new direction for Gilligan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.429
fans are still hoping for a return to&nbsp;
Albuquerque in the not-too-distant future.

00:08:29.429 --> 00:08:33.869
"Fantastic! Phew. That makes my life much easier."

